==English==

===Etymology===
From the {{uder|en|la|sine||without}} + {{m|la|diē}}, the ablative singular of {{m|la|diēs||a day}}. Literally meaning "without a day".

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsʌɪni ˈdʌɪiː/}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# [[without|Without]] [[specifying]] a [[date]] (for a [[future]] [[event]]); [[indefinitely]].
#*'''1860''', {{w|Ellen Wood}}, ''East Lynne'', Penguin 2005, p. 6:
#*:Years ago […] he might have retrieved his position; but he had done what most people will do in such cases—put off the evil day '''''sine die''''', and gone on increasing his enormous list of debts.
#* '''1979''', {{w|André Brink}}, ''A Dry White Season'', Vintage 1998, p. 105:
#*: In anticipation of Dr Hassiem's release the inquest of Mr Ngubene's death, originally scheduled for 13 April, was postponed '''''sine die'''''.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|adjournment sine die}}

